[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, so uh welcome to Ragdoll for web. Uh I\'ll assume no prior knowledge of Ragdoll in this video and I\'ll focus entirely on the features that are accessible via Ragdoll for web. Uh so, Ragdoll is a physically based 3D'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character animation tool. It was originally made in 2021 as a plugin for Autodesk Maya. Uh and since then we\'ve been working on a version of Ragdoll that is independent of of any'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'software really, like a standalone version. And uh what we\'re looking at now is a phase two out of seven out of this completed product. Um it only has a limited set of features that exists in our Maya plugin. And to'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get the basics out of the way, to navigate, you click anywhere on the canvas to rotate, middle click to pan, and to move the camera towards and away using the left mouse button. Uh if you'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have no middle mouse button, you can also hold shift and left press to to pan. And I mentioned it\'s a physically based animation tool. And that means that you can do normal'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things like rotate the arm or rotate the fingers, but you can also do things like pull on the arm and have the rest of the character follow along and also for other characters to interact with the'

[01:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whatever is coming to contact with them. So, for example, if I were to drag this hand around and give him give him a big hug, I can sort of create tension by just squeezing the characters'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together. Uh it also means that you can press this little button at the bottom or just hit space to let the simulation sort of just run, like you just apply gravity onto the characters. And even while gravity'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is being applied, you can still interact with the characters to create um sort of in-flight poses. For for if you wanted to establish a very dynamic pose, you can throw him a little bit'

[02:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then go back in time to where where you thought it looked the most interesting. Like this, for example. So, now you have a pose of him like if you disregard what I just did and just'

[02:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'imagine this pose on its own, it\'s like he got pushed out of a window or is falling off a ledge. Like you can sort of tell that there\'s there\'s motion happening even in this in this still frame. So, if you were to use this, for'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'example, as in your sort of illustration as a reference, uh this pose would have captured a lot of that energy that came from that throw. And the other thing you notice that uh whenever you drag or hit play, uh the timeline starts'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moving. And uh that\'s because everything that you do inside of Raddle is being essentially recorded onto this timeline. So, if you uh if you make a mistake or if you\'re something that you\'re not'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'happy with, you can click and drag on it and it will just take you back in time. So, you see here here even the sort of the the mouse drags that I\'m doing on this arm uh is sort of showing up as past events that uh you\'re scrubbing'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through. All right. So, let\'s take a step back and let me just uh start fresh. So, the first thing you\'ll see when you visit the site is going to be this uh this UI here this this welcome UI. And uh it will have uh two options at'

[03:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the moment. One is to open the thing you saved last, which is going to be grayed out, obviously, because this is the first time you open it. And then it\'s to start fresh, which is just like a default uh mostly empty scene, which is this'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh default character that we have. And I mentioned navigation. Uh right now I\'m I\'m still holding Alt as I navigate because I\'m just used to that. And if you are used to that, too, you can turn off Alt-free navigation. And uh that will just give you the benefit of being'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'able to navigate whilst holding your cursor over the character. Uh especially useful if you\'re, say, posing the character from from down here near the ground, uh but there is no empty space to to navigate with. Holding'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Alt key just bypasses all of that and makes it a bit more uh predictable. Uh another thing that will be uh enabled by default is these hints. Well, they look like this, so I\'m going'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to sort of fake their appearance. Uh it\'s a little blue box that shows up at the right time. So, when you when you start posing, when you start dragging or clicking a button, uh some of these hints might show up. So, this one tells you about a a post library that\'s at the'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bottom here. Uh it\'s useful for if you have a a post that you like, uh but you want to sort of continue to explore other poses. You can then click here and it will store that pose in a uh sort of a slot. So, now as you uh make further'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'changes to your character, let\'s say uh you have this now, you can then store this pose over here and then go back to the first one. Another useful thing to be aware of is that um by default when you click and drag on a'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'limb, it\'ll rotate that limb, but it will also uh affect the uh the rest of the character, the the parent and the grandparents. And uh that\'s because, like I mentioned, it\'s uh it\'s a physically it\'s a'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simulation, so you\'re just applying a force onto the arm, and because the torso is attached to the arm, the the torso will follow, and so everything else attached to to that. If you only wanted to rotate the arm, and you wanted to leave the the parent where it is, uh'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you could hold the the Shift key. So, when I hold the Shift key and drag, the the parent will not move. And that\'s typically how you would pose if you want these sort of more finer grained uh tweaks to the character.'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And uh yeah, when while I\'m at it, you can see how I\'m I\'m using the uh the top part of the arm to twist it as well. Uh cuz I like I mentioned, like the force is applied where you click. So, if you click down here, uh it\'s going to'

[05:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'act as though you sort of grabbed this part with your finger and pulled it around. And if you click on this little you know, on the ball joint, on the on the sockets, on the you know, on the joint of each limb, you\'re applying it onto the sphere,'

[05:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basically, and the sphere becomes like a little trackball that you can then use to to primarily twist the arm. Uh the next thing you might want to know is that once you\'re sort of happy with the way a particular limb looks, like'

[06:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s say you you wanted the feet to uh remain planted on the ground in this particular uh configuration. Uh then you can control-click and it will mask the the limb that you click.'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And so now when you drag on other body parts, uh the the legs will sort of or the feet, rather, will stay where they are. Another useful thing is if I unmask these feet, um when you drag on a body part, you'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will move it and you hold shift to rotate. Uh the same thing applies to the hip. So when I drag the hip, it\'s basically translating the whole character. Uh it\'s especially useful if you have, you know, multiple characters.'

[06:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So um let me just hit tab and bring in another character just so we can demonstrate. I\'m going to append a character to the scene. Uh so now when you drag the hip on on either of these, you can then uh also'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hold shift to rotate. Uh and then I just place them together in the scene like this. So that helps you to do the uh sort of the blocking layout of the characters. So you can see how when I\'m dragging towards the ground, it also,'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you know, I\'m using the ground as basically a a pivot. So you can come into contact with the ground, give it some sort of force pushing down, and then you can just drag it around to establish a an orientation that still'

[07:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leaves them resting on the ground. All right, let\'s come back to the scene for a moment and uh let me show you the tooltips. So as you\'d expect, when you hover any widget, you\'ll get a like a'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little description of what it does. And uh also, as the tooltip says, if you hold the control key or command on Mac, you\'ll get a a video sort of demonstrating what it does, like a short 2-second clip. And you can just keep'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'holding control and it will show you the the videos for the various widgets. Uh and next let\'s go through the the widgets on this side actually. Um we\'ve been using the rotate tool almost'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exclusively throughout this video and it\'s where you you click and drag to to rotate. Uh but the the simpler one, the one that doesn\'t do it only applies the force and it does not care for the the child hierarchy. Uh'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because like I mentioned rotate will preserve the orientation of all the children in the hierarchy. Uh pinch does not do that. So when I drag on on the limb now you can see how all the the limbs they sort of'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they just go with the flow. Um and that can be useful for making tweaks to to limbs that are uh heavily connected. So like the the the the pelvis is a good example. If you want to move just the pelvis,'

[08:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh then pinch is your go-to tool. So now when you drag on the pelvis it will affect uh only the pelvis and not care about the hierarchy. Uh because again if you use rotate and drag on the pelvis, it will'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'affect the entire hierarchy. Uh next one is move and that does what you think it does. It just takes any body part that you drag and just moves it. So it preserves the orientation of'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what you drag uh including the child hierarchy. Uh and if you hold shift, it will lock the the parent. Uh next one is pin. So pin will just apply a pin to any body part. So now when you either hit play'

[09:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for example or when you uh drag with any of the tools, it will just preserve the uh the location of that. That can be useful for like you know, if you want to rotate say around the head, the entire character or'

[09:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just preserve the position uh of a particular limb. Uh attach is uh it\'s like a pin but it is between two body parts. So let\'s say for example you wanted the uh the hand to rest on his uh head. You want to maintain the distance'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between the head and the hand. Attach or leave any blue to click and drag between two parts and then they\'ll those parts will then stay stay connected. So like as you move the character around it will sort of'

[09:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'maintain that that distance. Our next one is drop. Drop applies a downwards force, kind of like gravity, but only on the body part that you select. So if you select say the um'

[10:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the foot then only the foot gets gravity. But because the foot is attached to the lower leg, it will then drag the leg down with it. If you drag on the the leg itself, it will include the child hierarchy.'

[10:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And if you drag on like the pelvis, it will just apply gravity to the whole character. It\'s kind of like he\'s falling and because the the because the attach is keeping the head and hand together, he\'s sort of'

[10:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pulling the guy\'s hair. Our next one is smooth and smooth will attempt to restore a body part to its rigged orientation. And that can be especially useful for things like uh'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well, primarily fingers. Like anything with a lot of of joints. So let me let me bring in a character with fingers just so we can uh have a look at what that looks like. So in this case, let\'s say we have a'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character and he\'s doing a a wave. And [snorts] let\'s say you had some fingers that you you know you you were say unhappy with for whatever for whatever reason. But if you wanted'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to straighten these fingers, you just want to get back to where things were, hold shift to prevent the parent from moving and just drag and it will restore the fingers into its uh default sort of rigged pose.'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s very similar to the next to the one down here, which is restore. Restore is the same except it it does it in world space instead of local space. So if I drag on the on the hand now, you can see how you will move the hand back'

[11:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to where it was rigged. The the ones that we skipped, so one that we\'ve already seen, mask, is the one that\'s being toggled whenever you hold the control key. Uh force is uh a bit of work in progress, so take this'

[11:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a grain of salt, but you can click on a body part and apply this line. Uh and now, as soon as you hit play, it will apply that force. Uh'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; so, then you can sort of go back in time and then you can choose a frame where you you know, where it was interesting. Uh the force is dependent on the sort of size of the object. So, if you have a heavy object, the force is going to have'

[12:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a stronger effect. And so, now for the chest, for example, it\'s going to be quite strong. So, now it\'s like he\'s he got shot. Whoa. And the last one is the light gizmo, and that\'s just to control'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the the light. So, if you drag on, say, the head and you want the head to have a shadow right over here, that\'s what you would use that for. So, let\'s say that you\'re'

[12:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re happy with your work and you want to you want to sort of save it so you can return to it later. Your natural inclination to go file save is is correct. You would just go file save and it does save the scene. The save is entirely local to your machine,'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so there\'s nothing being saved anywhere else. Like, this whole application runs on your machine. And the file that\'s being saved is being saved in your browser session. So, like, if you have'

[13:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Chrome and Firefox, for example, they would not share the save file. They would have their own save file. So, it\'s it\'s very limited right now. You only have one save and, you know, one thing to open. So, if I were to you know, change this, for example, and'

[13:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then go file open, it would open the thing you saved last. So, that\'s it\'s pretty primitive, but that\'s that\'s where we are. Uh some other options that you have, like, let\'s say we bring the character back down,'

[13:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh is these uh toggles up here. So, uh one of them is the composition flags. It gives you some some layouts that you can choose from like the A4 might be useful for like a social media post, for example.'

[13:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Uh, you got the movie format or the cinematic format uh and so on. You got some toggles for uh lighting. So, this one is whether the camera uh whether the light should be attached to the camera. So, now the the light is just not attached so the'

[13:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shadows will also remain where they are. The other toggles are default material just making it all the same color. You got the perspective versus uh orthographic which also is, you know, can be quite useful.'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the grid different shading modes including a wireframe look uh like a simpler shading or a matcap based shading. So, you have different options here. And uh finally'

[14:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sort of the physically based one which is the default. It just gives you these basic roughness, metallic toggles. All right. Well, that\'s it for this demo. So, uh I hope'

[14:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you enjoyed it and uh I mean, stay tuned for the upcoming releases. We\'re going to uh fix some of the things obviously that you found that was broken. But, uh mostly we\'re going to add uh key framing as next and after that uh recording uh'

